WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:09.017
<v Ken>A hundred and four point nine seconds, by pencil. A hundred and three point one, by the sum. A hundred and two point four, on the clock.

00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:10.644
<v Bev>And which one's right, Ken?

00:00:10.924 --> 00:00:13.448
<v Ken>The clock. The other two are estimates.

00:00:24.248 --> 00:00:31.469
<v Bev>Hello there, it's Bev, the kettle's on, and this is Dalex After Hours, the slot where we find out how computer games are really made.

00:00:32.069 --> 00:00:37.010
<v Bev>Every fact on this show comes out of the book the team's writing, and from the games themselves.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.268
<v Bev>Gareth's with me, as ever.

00:00:39.347 --> 00:00:40.100
<v Gareth>Hiya, Bev.

00:00:40.221 --> 00:00:50.280
<v Bev>And tonight's special guest has come all the way down from the top floor. He's opened his door, which I'm told is rare. It's Ken Ramsbottom, from Accounts. Hello, Ken.

00:00:50.454 --> 00:00:54.618
<v Ken>Good evening, Bev. Good evening, Gareth. Humbug, anyone?

00:00:54.759 --> 00:00:55.997
<v Bev>Ooh, go on then.

00:00:56.025 --> 00:00:57.307
<v Gareth>Two, please, Ken.

00:00:57.407 --> 00:00:59.257
<v Ken>One each. I've counted them.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:10.198
<v Bev>Now Ken, the Pet Shop Boys went to number one on Sunday with Always on My Mind, and Rick Astley's at two. Give us an estimate for Christmas number one. With a range, mind.

00:01:10.346 --> 00:01:16.448
<v Ken>An estimate needs figures, Bev, and I've one week of them. I'd put it at the Pet Shop Boys, with Rick Astley inside the range.

00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:17.643
<v Bev>And if it's somebody else?

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.623
<v Ken>Then I'll write down why. Ask me again on Sunday, when there's a receipt.

00:01:24.923 --> 00:01:26.988
<v Bev>So what's up there on the top floor?

00:01:27.312 --> 00:01:34.691
<v Ken>Accounts. Upstairs, because accounts always are. A desk, a green ledger, an adding machine and some filing cabinets.

00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:37.742
<v Gareth>Everything has a folder. Even the folders.

00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:41.138
<v Ken>Even the folders. And I check the adding machine against myself.

00:01:41.108 --> 00:01:41.980
<v Bev>Does it ever win?

00:01:42.345 --> 00:01:45.452
<v Ken>We usually agree. When we don't, I find out why.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:50.476
<v Bev>They say downstairs that Ken is reconciling. Ken is always reconciling.

00:01:50.549 --> 00:01:51.627
<v Ken>It's a fair description.

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:55.193
<v Bev>What does reconciling mean, for those of us who just put it on the card?

00:01:55.482 --> 00:02:01.759
<v Ken>Two lists that ought to agree, checked against each other until they do. Or until you know exactly why they don't.

00:02:01.781 --> 00:02:03.236
<v Bev>And what's in the ledger?

00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:13.320
<v Ken>My ledger works out what the work would cost. The jobs that write the code and draw the pictures use up an allowance as they go, and the ledger estimates what that would cost if you paid for it by the piece, at the going rate.

00:02:13.573 --> 00:02:18.555
<v Gareth>And there's a red meter by the Production door. It shows how much of this week's allowance is used.

00:02:18.772 --> 00:02:20.559
<v Bev>So it's like the gas meter.

00:02:20.771 --> 00:02:32.281
<v Ken>A bit. But mind, I keep the two apart. The meter's a percentage of an allowance. The ledger's an estimate in money. I never add one to the other, and I never turn a percentage into pounds.

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:35.079
<v Bev>Why not? Wouldn't it be handy?

00:02:35.466 --> 00:02:43.141
<v Ken>Handy and wrong. You'd be adding apples to bus tickets. If two figures are worked out on different bases, they don't go in one total.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.321
<v Bev>Who do you get on with best in the building?

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:57.787
<v Ken>Mo, for budgets. She runs the board and I run the column, and we like a figure that adds up. And Derek, for precision. He signs slowly and I add slowly, and between us nothing gets past.

00:02:57.951 --> 00:03:02.600
<v Gareth>The two of them can go down a column together without a word. It's lovely to watch.

00:03:02.555 --> 00:03:03.335
<v Ken>It's restful.

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:06.856
<v Bev>Oh, Ken. Percy's just walked onto your ledger.

00:03:07.097 --> 00:03:11.924
<v Ken>Mind the pencil, Percy. He likes it because it's warm. I work round him.

00:03:12.163 --> 00:03:14.831
<v Bev>Right then, what's the one idea this week?

00:03:15.095 --> 00:03:23.961
<v Ken>An estimate is a shopping list. A measurement is the till receipt. You need both, and you write down both, and you never pass the list off as the receipt.

00:03:24.183 --> 00:03:27.542
<v Bev>So what's the difference, really? They're both numbers.

00:03:27.697 --> 00:03:44.002
<v Ken>The shopping list says what you think you'll spend. Bread, milk, a quarter of humbugs. The till receipt says what you did spend. If they're different, the list wasn't a lie. It was a list. The trouble only starts when someone hands you the list and calls it a receipt.

00:03:44.225 --> 00:03:46.904
<v Bev>That's the shopping list, the receipt comes later.

00:03:46.904 --> 00:03:48.115
<v Ken>That's my line, Bev.

00:03:48.121 --> 00:03:48.971
<v Bev>I've been listening.

00:03:49.134 --> 00:03:59.641
<v Ken>So you have. And the ledger is all shopping list. It says what the work would cost at the going rate. It's not a bill anyone's paid, it's not money in, and it's not profit.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:02.026
<v Bev>And what's that got to do with computer games?

00:04:02.402 --> 00:04:13.230
<v Gareth>Everything, it turns out. Before a game's written, we make a budget. A plan for how the time in one update gets shared out. So much for the keys, so much for the sprites, so much for the score.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:15.016
<v Bev>Like a household budget.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:25.776
<v Gareth>Exactly like one, except the money is time. On the hundred and twenty-eight K, if a game updates every two frames, it's got a hundred and forty-one thousand, eight hundred and sixteen clock ticks to spend on each update.

00:04:25.973 --> 00:04:31.512
<v Ken>And here's the rule I'd put on the front of every one. Every figure in the budget is a guess until the game measures it.

00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:33.251
<v Bev>And were the guesses any good?

00:04:33.553 --> 00:04:42.467
<v Gareth>Three of the games found out the hard way. Wordris guessed sixteen hundred ticks to draw one square of its well. It measured about three thousand nine hundred.

00:04:42.681 --> 00:04:44.483
<v Ken>Two point four times the estimate.

00:04:44.741 --> 00:04:50.899
<v Gareth>WOLF budgeted about eight thousand ticks for each character sprite. It measured fifteen to twenty-five thousand.

00:04:50.863 --> 00:04:51.684
<v Bev>Oh dear.

00:04:51.972 --> 00:04:59.606
<v Gareth>And Gold Master's first way of drawing looked up a screen address for every line of every picture. It spent about half of every update doing that.

00:04:59.861 --> 00:05:01.480
<v Bev>So were the games in trouble?

00:05:01.866 --> 00:05:13.013
<v Ken>Not one of them. That's what I like about this lot. None of the three was sunk by its guesses. Each one measured, adjusted and shipped. That's good bookkeeping, Bev, whatever they call it downstairs.

00:05:13.247 --> 00:05:15.739
<v Gareth>Ken told us that a few times while we were doing it.

00:05:15.806 --> 00:05:17.510
<v Ken>I meant it every time.

00:05:17.532 --> 00:05:19.216
<v Bev>Now you promised me sums.

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:40.225
<v Ken>I did. Pencil, please, and a bit of paper. This is the tape for Antigravity, the space station game. It's in six blocks. I'll read you their sizes in bytes. Nineteen. Six hundred and thirty-two. Three thousand two hundred and forty-six. Twenty-four hundred and seven. Three thousand four hundred and fifty-two. Four thousand seven hundred and twenty-seven.

00:05:40.522 --> 00:05:49.581
<v Bev>Hang on, hang on. Nineteen, six three two, three two four six, two four oh seven, three four five two, four seven two seven.

00:05:49.795 --> 00:05:54.423
<v Ken>Very good. Now add them down. If you want a go, pause us now.

00:05:57.648 --> 00:06:00.067
<v Bev>I've got fourteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-three.

00:06:00.264 --> 00:06:04.483
<v Ken>So have I. Now add them up. Start at the bottom.

00:06:04.434 --> 00:06:06.796
<v Bev>Up? I've just done it.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.577
<v Ken>Add it down, then add it up. If they agree, you can go home.

00:06:10.742 --> 00:06:18.274
<v Gareth>Four seven two seven and three four five two is eight one seven nine. Add the two four oh seven, ten thousand five hundred and eighty-six.

00:06:18.516 --> 00:06:29.189
<v Bev>Plus three two four six, thirteen thousand, eight hundred and thirty-two. Plus six three two. Fourteen thousand, four hundred and sixty-four. Plus nineteen.

00:06:29.182 --> 00:06:29.822
<v Ken>And?

00:06:30.134 --> 00:06:34.363
<v Bev>Fourteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-three. They agree!

00:06:34.492 --> 00:06:43.672
<v Ken>Then you can go home. But not yet. The tools write the whole tape out as one file first. That file is fourteen thousand, four hundred and ninety-five bytes.

00:06:43.812 --> 00:06:46.565
<v Bev>That's twelve more. Where've they gone?

00:06:46.891 --> 00:06:49.042
<v Ken>That's the question I like. Gareth?

00:06:49.401 --> 00:07:00.934
<v Gareth>Every block in the tape file has two bytes in front of it, saying how long the block is. Six blocks, two bytes each. Twelve. They're never played. They're the label on the drawer.

00:07:01.168 --> 00:07:09.195
<v Ken>So now the column reconciles. Fourteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-three on the tape, plus twelve, gives the file. Nothing missing, nothing extra.

00:07:09.201 --> 00:07:10.804
<v Bev>That's rather satisfying.

00:07:11.062 --> 00:07:12.302
<v Ken>It's the best part of the job.

00:07:14.802 --> 00:07:20.437
<v Bev>So that's Antigravity playing now. And what's the sum for, Ken? Why would I want to know the bytes?

00:07:20.698 --> 00:07:25.640
<v Ken>Because from the bytes you can estimate how long a player waits while it loads. Before you've loaded a thing.

00:07:25.904 --> 00:07:36.941
<v Gareth>Here's the estimate, the way the book does it. Fourteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-three bytes at the Spectrum's tape speed, assuming half the bits are ones. That's eighty-four point nine seconds of data.

00:07:37.226 --> 00:07:45.645
<v Ken>Then the leaders. The steady tone before each block. One header at five seconds, five data blocks at two seconds each. Fifteen seconds.

00:07:45.811 --> 00:07:49.993
<v Gareth>And a second's pause after every block but the last. Five seconds.

00:07:50.147 --> 00:07:58.682
<v Ken>Eighty-four point nine, fifteen and five. A hundred and four point nine seconds. About a minute and three quarters. That's the shopping list.

00:07:58.649 --> 00:08:00.086
<v Bev>And the receipt?

00:08:00.448 --> 00:08:14.822
<v Gareth>The tape writer in the toolkit counts every bit of every byte. It makes it a hundred and three point one. And the media kit has a recording of the tape loading, through the real Spectrum ROM, timed from start to finish. It measured a hundred and two point four.

00:08:15.088 --> 00:08:17.609
<v Bev>So the pencil was out by nearly two seconds.

00:08:17.848 --> 00:08:33.458
<v Ken>One point eight, high. And I can tell you why, which matters more than the size of it. The estimate assumed half the bits were ones. On this tape forty-seven per cent are. A nought costs half as much tape as a one, so a tape with fewer ones loads a little quicker.

00:08:33.723 --> 00:08:36.618
<v Bev>So you'd rather be wrong for a reason than right by luck.

00:08:36.911 --> 00:08:38.831
<v Ken>Bev, that's going in the ledger.

00:08:39.104 --> 00:08:46.537
<v Gareth>And it's one of the best things in the book, that. Three figures, three ways, and the team showing all three. Not hiding the gap.

00:08:46.714 --> 00:08:51.622
<v Ken>Exactly so. A good estimate comes with its working, and its working tells you where it went wrong.

00:08:51.855 --> 00:08:54.436
<v Bev>Does the toolkit tell everyone the load time, then?

00:08:54.819 --> 00:09:03.122
<v Gareth>Every build prints it. And the book's got a table of every game's load. Antigravity's is the quickest of the six, a hundred and three seconds.

00:09:03.266 --> 00:09:05.889
<v Ken>Well done Antigravity. Credit where it's due.

00:09:06.768 --> 00:09:08.871
<v Bev>Give me a story where the books didn't balance.

00:09:09.027 --> 00:09:20.332
<v Ken>I've a good one, and it's not money at all. Mower Mayhem's sixth milestone. Before a milestone passes, there's a gate. A separate review of all the evidence. The build, the checks, the playtests.

00:09:20.571 --> 00:09:29.653
<v Gareth>And the first thing it checks is that every piece of evidence is about the same tape. Every tape gets a fingerprint, a long number worked out from its bytes. They all have to match.

00:09:29.918 --> 00:09:39.745
<v Ken>Verify had passed on the tape. The last playtest had no serious problems open. Every tone check was clean. And it still failed.

00:09:39.828 --> 00:09:40.440
<v Bev>Why?

00:09:40.709 --> 00:09:47.750
<v Ken>One of the checks it needed had been run on an older tape. Since that run, nine source files had changed, including the code that moves the mower.

00:09:47.987 --> 00:09:50.226
<v Bev>So the receipt was for a different shop.

00:09:50.385 --> 00:09:55.845
<v Ken>The receipt was for a different shop. Very good, Bev. You'd make an accountant.

00:09:56.011 --> 00:10:04.210
<v Gareth>And the verdict said exactly what to do. Run that check again, on this tape. So the team did. It passed thirteen out of thirteen.

00:10:04.398 --> 00:10:11.719
<v Ken>And then the gate did a thing I admire. It reran the check itself, from a clean copy, and got a byte for byte identical result before it accepted it.

00:10:11.765 --> 00:10:13.182
<v Bev>It checked the checker.

00:10:13.452 --> 00:10:17.631
<v Ken>Down, then up. Same answer. Then you can go home.

00:10:17.849 --> 00:10:25.226
<v Gareth>The re-gate gave it a pass with issues. Nobody had done anything wrong, really. The evidence just needed to be about the tape we meant to ship.

00:10:25.405 --> 00:10:33.262
<v Ken>And nobody minded being asked. That's the thing about this building. You point at a column that doesn't balance, and three people come and help you balance it.

00:10:33.517 --> 00:10:35.153
<v Bev>Does it happen with your own ledger?

00:10:35.497 --> 00:10:43.276
<v Ken>Oh, the ledger has its rules too. If a day's records are missing, that day stays unknown. I don't fill it in with a guess.

00:10:43.237 --> 00:10:44.750
<v Bev>Not even an average?

00:10:45.014 --> 00:10:57.707
<v Ken>Not even an average. An unknown is a fact. A guess dressed as a figure is a fib. And if work's run on a machine here with no bill attached, I don't write it down as free. No charge doesn't mean no cost.

00:10:57.882 --> 00:11:06.435
<v Gareth>And the meter by the door has a rule I love. It cuts the number down, never rounds it up. So it never shows you past a warning line you haven't actually crossed.

00:11:06.404 --> 00:11:07.720
<v Bev>Warning lines?

00:11:08.038 --> 00:11:12.881
<v Gareth>Seventy-five per cent of the week's allowance is a warning. Ninety is danger.

00:11:13.068 --> 00:11:17.773
<v Ken>And if you're on seventy-four point nine, you're not on seventy-five. I won't round.

00:11:17.729 --> 00:11:18.909
<v Bev>Not even point one?

00:11:19.086 --> 00:11:20.159
<v Ken>Not for anybody.

00:11:20.418 --> 00:11:22.910
<v Bev>Can I make a budget, then? A game one?

00:11:23.272 --> 00:11:34.792
<v Gareth>Go on, then. The book's got a tiny game it calls Apple Catch. A basket and up to three apples. Two frames an update, so a hundred and forty-one thousand, eight hundred and sixteen ticks.

00:11:34.946 --> 00:11:40.990
<v Ken>And you borrow the other games' first guesses, because you've nothing measured yet. Read them out, Gareth, and I'll keep the column.

00:11:41.206 --> 00:11:54.098
<v Gareth>The interrupt, for the keys and the music, twenty thousand. Input, six hundred. Logic, fifteen hundred. Four sprites at eight thousand each. The score display, three thousand. And a margin of eight thousand.

00:11:54.334 --> 00:11:59.670
<v Ken>Sixty-five thousand, one hundred. Fits with room to spare. On paper.

00:11:59.959 --> 00:12:03.068
<v Bev>On paper. I can hear it in your voice, Ken.

00:12:03.328 --> 00:12:08.467
<v Ken>Now put in what WOLF actually measured for a sprite. Twenty-five thousand at worst, four of them.

00:12:08.702 --> 00:12:13.956
<v Gareth>A hundred thousand for sprites. The total's a hundred and thirty-three thousand, one hundred.

00:12:14.212 --> 00:12:20.488
<v Ken>Which still fits two frames, with eight thousand, seven hundred and sixteen to spare. But try it at one frame an update and it doesn't fit at all.

00:12:20.737 --> 00:12:22.576
<v Bev>So the guess said either would do.

00:12:22.776 --> 00:12:37.313
<v Gareth>And the two scrolling games show where it bites. Tiger Gorilla planned three frames an update. A camera move cost it two hundred thousand to two hundred and thirty thousand ticks, and three frames only gave it two hundred and twelve thousand, seven hundred and twenty-four.

00:12:37.258 --> 00:12:38.698
<v Ken>So the worst ones overran.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:47.355
<v Gareth>Twenty-three per cent of its play updates. So it moved to four frames, and then six hundred and ninety-nine of its seven hundred and two updates took exactly four.

00:12:47.544 --> 00:12:51.901
<v Ken>Six hundred and ninety-nine out of seven hundred and two. Now that's a figure I can file.

00:12:52.151 --> 00:12:59.788
<v Gareth>WOLF went to four frames as well, but its worst scrolling update was three hundred and ninety thousand ticks. That's five and a half frames.

00:12:59.861 --> 00:13:01.802
<v Bev>So four wasn't enough either.

00:13:02.087 --> 00:13:15.040
<v Gareth>So it cut the worst case down instead. At most two characters walking at once, and the camera moving in bursts. Its scrolling scenes average four point one frames an update, and the team wrote that down as the compromise it accepted.

00:13:15.339 --> 00:13:23.974
<v Ken>That's what I respect. Pick from the worst case, not the average. And when you can't fit the worst case, say what you accepted. Out loud, in writing.

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:26.847
<v Bev>You'd have liked them, Ken. Writing it down.

00:13:26.983 --> 00:13:31.796
<v Ken>I do like them, Bev. They're a very tidy lot downstairs, whatever the state of their desks.

00:13:32.396 --> 00:13:41.550
<v Ken>And the measurement decides. Measure one sprite first, in the real tape, and then set your budget. That's the shopping list checked against one real receipt before you fill the trolley.

00:13:41.774 --> 00:13:53.308
<v Gareth>And Wordris did that beautifully. It planned and measured each of its three modes. Two came in under. One came in over by eight thousand two hundred, and still fitted, because the budget had left room.

00:13:53.593 --> 00:13:56.557
<v Ken>That's a team that kept a margin. I'd sign that.

00:13:59.857 --> 00:14:04.108
<v Bev>Go on, Ken, crystal ball time. What will your ledger look like in the future?

00:14:04.361 --> 00:14:08.614
<v Ken>I don't do guesses, Bev. I do estimates.

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:10.879
<v Bev>Then give us an estimate of the future.

00:14:11.055 --> 00:14:19.794
<v Ken>With a range, mind. Suppose one day you could hire a computer to write the program for you. You'd type what you wanted, in plain English, and it would type the code back.

00:14:19.996 --> 00:14:28.126
<v Gareth>And suppose it was charged by the word. Every word it read and every word it wrote, a tiny fraction of a penny each, and an allowance for the week.

00:14:28.425 --> 00:14:35.201
<v Ken>Then somebody would need a ledger for it. Words in, words out, at the going rate. And I'd still keep the allowance apart from the money.

00:14:35.366 --> 00:14:40.159
<v Bev>Like on Tomorrow's World. A computer that writes the code, and Ken upstairs adding it up.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.385
<v Ken>Some things don't change, Bev. The column still has to balance.

00:14:43.511 --> 00:14:46.039
<v Bev>What should folk do before next week?

00:14:46.369 --> 00:14:56.103
<v Gareth>The book the team's writing has a chapter called Shipping: Tapes, Loading Screens and the Box. Its second exercise is called Estimate a whole tape.

00:14:56.121 --> 00:14:56.625
<v Bev>Go on.

00:14:56.778 --> 00:15:09.445
<v Gareth>It gives you Mower Mayhem's tape, block by block. Nineteen bytes, six hundred and twenty-five, three thousand nine hundred and seventy-two, four thousand eight hundred and seventy-nine and twelve thousand. You estimate the load time the way we've just done.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:13.761
<v Ken>You should get about a hundred and forty-three seconds. The build prints a hundred and forty-one.

00:15:13.783 --> 00:15:15.413
<v Bev>And if they're different?

00:15:15.766 --> 00:15:22.022
<v Ken>Then write down why. Estimates, mind. Not invoices.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:23.979
<v Gareth>And add it down and up first.

00:15:23.948 --> 00:15:24.930
<v Ken>Always.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:36.813
<v Bev>That's us for this week. And both Manchester clubs won on Saturday. United three one against Oxford, and City one nil at Millwall.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:37.882
<v Ken>I estimated that.

00:15:37.973 --> 00:15:38.851
<v Bev>Did you, Ken?

00:15:39.132 --> 00:15:42.208
<v Ken>No. But it would have been a good estimate.

00:15:42.365 --> 00:15:56.263
<v Bev>Next time it's the last of the season, and it's a hands-on one. Gareth and Yvonne are going to make one small change to a game, really build it and really play it. If you've got a question for the team, or for Ken, send it in, and we'll put it to them.

00:15:56.863 --> 00:15:58.015
<v Bev>Thanks, Ken.

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:02.404
<v Ken>Thank you, Bev. There's one humbug left. I'll put it on the ledger.

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:03.671
<v Bev>Right then, turn it up.

00:16:03.803 --> 00:16:18.065
<v Daniel>This is Daniel Vaughan, who made the show. That was nineteen eighty-seven. Ken's estimate of the future was a good one. The jobs that write the code are AI agents, the allowance is their weekly limit, and the red meter shows how much of it is used.

00:16:18.665 --> 00:16:36.457
<v Daniel>The chapter is Shipping: Tapes, Loading Screens and the Box, chapter seventeen in Machine Code Mayhem on Leanpub, and the exercise is Estimate a whole tape. Ken would want the sum checked against a real load, in an emulator such as Fuse at normal speed.

00:16:37.057 --> 00:16:46.300
<v Daniel>Thank you for listening. The theme is by Dez, the characters are fictional, the voices are synthesised, and the show is written and produced by me, Daniel Vaughan.
